  "lessons": [
    {
      "domain_id": "hermetic-principles",
      "title": "Hermetic Principles \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Close reading: the careful reading is structural analogy: patterns at one scale illuminate patterns at another (microcosm/macrocosm). The honest reading asks what the claim meant to its authors and what it does in their system.",
        "Analogy vs identity: collapsing analogy into identity is how careful metaphysics becomes sloppy mysticism; holding the distinction is the whole critical skill.",
        "Philosophical analysis: the critical move is neither belief nor dismissal but analysis \u2014 what would follow if it were true, what evidence could bear on it, where it begs the question."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "Paraphrase of the Emerald Tablet's famous line: 'That which is above is as that which is below.' (Public-domain translations vary.)",
        "walkthrough": "The careful reading is structural analogy: patterns at one scale illuminate patterns at another (microcosm/macrocosm). 'As' signals correspondence, not identity \u2014 the text doesn't claim a planet IS an atom."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: that mind or perception is primary in the account of reality.",
        "Common trap: that everything in the universe is literally identical.",
        "Common trap: that the tablet is a recipe for transmuting lead.",
        "Common trap: that ancient authors understood neuroscience."
      ],
      "try_it": "Work through this in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "enochian-system",
      "title": "Enochian System \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Source criticism starts here \u2014 we have Dee's records of Kelley's reports, not direct access to anything else.",
        "Systematicity: whether one 'uses' it or studies it, the systematicity is the datum: somebody built (or received) a complete machine, not a pile of parts.",
        "System analysis: fixed sequence + cosmological mapping = a navigable architecture: each Call addresses a specific region of the system's universe, in order.",
        "Transmission reshapes: the Golden Dawn's Enochian is Dee's material refracted through Victorian magical theory."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "The Enochian corpus includes a complete alphabet, vocabulary, and grammar fragments, recorded systematically across years.",
        "walkthrough": "A complete, internally consistent script appearing in a few years is remarkable whether one attributes it to angels, the unconscious, or fraud \u2014 each hypothesis must explain the systematicity. Linguists note its structures; historians note the delivery method. The interesting question isn't just 'real or fake' but 'what kind of human (or other) process produces systematic novelty like this?'."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: it's a constructed script with its own alphabet, delivered as a received system.",
        "Common trap: tables, squares, and letter-grids function as a notation: positions encode relations, like coordinates or code.",
        "Common trap: dee channeled everything alone in trance.",
        "Common trap: it's a naturally evolved language like English."
      ],
      "try_it": "Work through this in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "cybernetics-hyperstition",
      "title": "Cybernetics & Hyperstition \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Feedback loops: hyperstition: a fiction that makes itself real through the feedback loops it triggers \u2014 the bank-run rumor, the self-fulfilling investment thesis, the manifesto that recruits its own movement.",
        "Second-order cybernetics notes that social systems contain their own observers: predictions alter behavior (self-fulfilling or self-defeating prophecies), measurements disturb. It's second-order cybernetics applied to narrative: stories as causal agents.",
        "Reflexivity: the critical reader asks what the fiction illuminates (reflexivity, anticipation) without mistaking the device for a discovery.",
        "Hyperstition concept: the concept earns its keep wherever beliefs move markets, politics, or code \u2014 which is to say, everywhere social. The honest use of hyperstition marks this boundary explicitly.",
        "Theory-fiction: a philosophy collective that treated theory-fiction, jungle music, and cybernetics as one continuous practice \u2014 deliberately dissolving the line between analyzing systems and performing them."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "Second-order cybernetics: the observer is part of the system observed.",
        "walkthrough": "Second-order cybernetics notes that social systems contain their own observers: predictions alter behavior (self-fulfilling or self-defeating prophecies), measurements disturb. The thermostat is first-order \u2014 observed from outside. The market is second-order \u2014 the map redraws the territory."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: a CCRU-associated motif: time as non-linear, with the future leaking into the present.",
        "Common trap: that time travel is physically proven.",
        "Common trap: it includes the observer in the system: the act of observing/modeling changes what's observed.",
        "Common trap: accept every hyperstitional claim as true."
      ],
      "try_it": "Work through this in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "symbol-systems",
      "title": "Symbol Systems \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Arbitrariness of the sign: the sign works by social convention, not natural resemblance.",
        "Literacy: a tiny reusable sign-set collapses the cost of literacy: anyone can learn 26 shapes, so reading stops being a scribal monopoly.",
        "Notation externalizes cognition: positional numerals, algebraic symbols, and dy/dx turn mental operations into visual manipulations you can perform reliably. Technologies of notation aren't neutral containers; they rewire what their users can think.",
        "Contemporary symbols: emoji work like rebus pictographs: context-flexible symbols that modulate alphabetic text \u2014 marking tone, softening requests, disambiguating intent."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "Paraphrase: the linguistic sign unites a sound-image and a concept; their bond is arbitrary.",
        "walkthrough": "Nothing about t-r-ee resembles a tree \u2014 Spanish says \u00e1rbol, Japanese says ki. This arbitrariness is what makes language flexible (any meaning can attach to any sound) and what makes translation lossy (conventions differ). Every symbol system \u2014 including code \u2014 inherits this: variable names mean by agreement."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: it democratized literacy: a small set of reusable signs (20-odd letters) can encode any utterance, versus thousands of memorized glyphs.",
        "Common trap: psychologically: condensing an intention into a symbol gives the mind a handle.",
        "Common trap: notation is just shorthand with no cognitive role.",
        "Common trap: emoji function like rebus writing: pictographs carrying flexible, context-dependent meaning."
      ],
      "try_it": "Work through this in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "critical-reading",
      "title": "Critical Reading \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Historical semantics is the entry fee for old texts \u2014 check the OED's dated senses, read contemporaries, and never trust a familiar word.",
        "Translation criticism: source criticism doesn't disprove the teachings' value \u2014 it correctly prices the lineage claim.",
        "Genre decides the reading stance: a ritual manual's ascent is a visualization protocol; a cosmology's is a truth claim; an allegory's is a moral map.",
        "Source criticism: compare translations, consult the source term, and treat translator disagreement as information about conceptual mismatch, not noise.",
        "Anachronism is evidence: the live hypotheses are later interpolation, misdating, or forgery (or a genuinely independent anticipation \u2014 rare, and needing strong proof)."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "A 17th-century text praises 'enthusiasm' \u2014 then a positive term for divine inspiration.",
        "walkthrough": "Recover period meaning first: 'enthusiasm' then meant divine inspiration, not naive overexcitement. Reading with modern definitions manufactures nonsense the author never wrote."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: determine the genre first: cosmology, allegory, ritual manual, and polemic each demand different reading stances.",
        "Common trap: keep sources distinct: map each claim to its source, note agreements and tensions, and let contradictions stand as data.",
        "Common trap: pick your favorite source and ignore the rest.",
        "Common trap: pick the translation that sounds best."
      ],
      "try_it": "Work through this in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "esoteric-programming",
      "title": "Esoteric Programming \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Control flow: the program counter gains a direction on a plane: > sends it right, v down, and control flow becomes literal geometry \u2014 loops are circuits, branches are forks in a maze.",
        "Spatial computing: it exposes how much ordinary programming leans on the hidden convention 'next = down one line.' Befunge makes sequence spatial, and in doing so reveals sequence was always a choice.",
        "Parody is analysis by caricature: by pushing design tics past the line, INTERCAL maps exactly where the line is.",
        "Naming: SPL literalizes what good programmers know: naming is casting."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "Shakespeare Programming Language: variables are characters ('Romeo', 'Juliet'); assignment is dialogue.",
        "walkthrough": "SPL literalizes what good programmers know: naming is casting. 'Romeo' carrying a value through scenes makes vividly clear that identifiers are roles in a drama of state \u2014 the program is a play, execution a performance. The esolang's joke is a genuine insight: code is read by humans performing it in their heads, and names are the cast list."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: parody as critique: INTERCAL's 'features' (politeness modifiers, line-noise operators) satirize real language design tics by exaggeration.",
        "Common trap: control flow becomes geometry: the instruction pointer moves on a 2D grid, redirected by arrow commands.",
        "Common trap: both are precise utterances where exact repetition matters: a mispronounced word or mistyped character breaks the operation.",
        "Common trap: constraints as engines: Oulipo-style rules and esolang restrictions force invention."
      ],
      "try_it": "Work through this in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "hermetic-principles",
      "title": "The Seven Principles as a System of Interpretation",
      "concepts": [
        "The seven principles come to us from The Kybalion (1908), published under the byline 'Three Initiates' and commonly attributed by scholars to William Walker Atkinson. They belong to the early-20th-century occult revival, not to the ancient Corpus Hermeticum. This is not a demotion: it simply tells us what kind of source we are reading \u2014 modern interpretive tradition \u2014 so we can study it honestly.",
        "Mentalism ('THE ALL is MIND; The Universe is Mental') is presented as the master key: the claim that reality is fundamentally mental, and that disciplined mind is therefore the first instrument of the student. Every other principle is read through this root.",
        "Correspondence ('As above, so below') is a method of analogical reading: patterns on one plane illuminate patterns on another. Vibration ('Nothing rests; everything moves') universalizes motion; Polarity ('opposites are identical in nature, but different in degree') structures it as a scale; Rhythm ('everything flows, out and in') describes its swing; Cause and Effect ('chance is but a name for law not recognized') denies causeless events; Gender describes generation through the union of projective and receptive principles on all planes.",
        "The tradition insists the principles interlock: Correspondence without Mentalism is fortune-telling by analogy; Polarity without Rhythm is a static scale; Causation without Correspondence cannot read the chain across planes. Study them as one instrument with seven registers, not seven separate tools.",
        "A standing caution: the 1908 text predates the mature quantum theory it is sometimes said to anticipate, and modern commentary that merges the principles with physics is a 20th\u201321st-century phenomenon worth studying as commentary \u2014 not as evidence of what the text itself establishes.",
        "The practical posture of the Kybalion tradition is contemplative rather than manipulative: observe the principle in experience, trace its operation, and work with its law rather than against it. The exercises in this path follow that posture \u2014 journaling, symbolic reading, and slow analysis rather than any claim of supernatural technique."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "\"Like and unlike are the same; opposites are identical in nature, but different in degree; extremes meet; all truths are but half-truths; all paradoxes may be reconciled.\" \u2014 The Kybalion (1908), the axiom of Polarity",
        "walkthrough": "Work the axiom clause by clause. 'Opposites are identical in nature, but different in degree' is the core: fear and courage are not two substances but one scale. 'Extremes meet' follows: push far enough along the scale and you arrive at the far pole \u2014 the tradition's basis for transmutation of mental states. 'All truths are but half-truths' is the epistemic humility the principle demands: any one pole is only half the scale. A correct contemplative use: locate your current state on the scale (say, at 'fear'), acknowledge it as a degree rather than a destiny, and practice moving the degree \u2014 the tradition's 'sliding' along the pole \u2014 rather than declaring war on an enemy."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Treating the principles as ancient: they are 1908 interpretive tradition; attributing them to Hermes Trismegistus himself is historically false.",
        "Literalizing Correspondence into mechanical determinism ('the stars control my liver') \u2014 the tradition means analogical likeness, not physical causation.",
        "Merging Vibration with modern physics and calling it 'confirmed' \u2014 analogy is not confirmation, and the chronology runs the wrong way.",
        "Reading Gender as biological essentialism \u2014 the text's own framing distinguishes the metaphysical principles of generation from physical sex.",
        "Using Polarity as positive thinking: the principle is about the structure of opposites, not about denying the low end of the scale."
      ],
      "try_it": "In the Terminal view, type: journal polarity \u2014 one fear you carry, where it sits on its scale, and one concrete degree-shift you could practice this week. Then reflect: what changed in the fear when you described it as a degree rather than a verdict?",
      "domain_id_remapped_from": "seven-principles",
      "merged_from": "cbt-individual/hermetica"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "hermetic-principles",
      "title": "From Alexandria to the Renaissance: A History of the Hermetic Texts",
      "concepts": [
        "Hermes Trismegistus is a syncretic figure \u2014 the Hellenistic fusion of the Greek Hermes and the Egyptian Thoth, gods of writing, wisdom, and mediation \u2014 not a single documented historical author. The Hermetica are a composite literature of the Greco-Egyptian world, attributed to his authority.",
        "The Corpus Hermeticum is a collection of seventeen Greek treatises (plus the Latin Asclepius, a dialogue surviving in Latin, and fragments preserved by Stobaeus). Its most famous treatise, the Poimandres (CH I), presents a vision received by Hermes from the divine Mind \u2014 a cosmogony in the service of gnosis, the direct knowing by which the soul ascends.",
        "Isaac Casaubon's 1614 demonstration remains the hinge of Hermetic scholarship: the Corpus belongs to the 1st\u20133rd centuries CE, not to a Mosaic antiquity. The redating moved the texts from legend into history \u2014 the real world of Roman Egypt, where Greek philosophy met Egyptian religion \u2014 and made them seriously studiable.",
        "The Emerald Tablet's documentary trail begins not in an Egyptian tomb but in Arabic sources (the Kit\u0101b Sirr al-Khal\u012bqa, c. 8th\u20139th century) and medieval Latin translations. Its thirteen lines gave alchemy its most quoted maxim and closed with the 'threefold wisdom' \u2014 read in tradition as alchemy, astrology, and theurgy.",
        "The Renaissance recovery ran through Florence: Cosimo de' Medici's 1460 manuscript and Marsilio Ficino's Latin translation (printed 1471) made Hermes a household name in learned Europe, feeding Renaissance Platonism, the prisca theologia framework, and early modern natural philosophy \u2014 Paracelsus in medicine, John Dee in mathematics and angel magic, the Rosicrucian manifestos from 1614.",
        "The modern line runs through the 19th-century occult revival \u2014 Eliphas L\u00e9vi, the Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn (1888), New Thought \u2014 and culminates for our purposes in The Kybalion (1908). The responsible student keeps the layers distinct: late-antique primary texts, medieval alchemical classics, Renaissance reception, and modern interpretation are different strata of one tradition."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "\"That which is below is like that which is above, and that which is above is like that which is below, to accomplish the miracles of the one thing.\" \u2014 the Emerald Tablet, in Isaac Newton's English rendering of the Latin",
        "walkthrough": "Read the sentence as the tradition reads it: a claim of likeness ('like'), not identity or causation. The 'miracles of the one thing' names the tablet's alchemy: the one matter worked through its correspondences. Historically, place the line correctly: you are reading a medieval Latin text translated from Arabic sources (8th\u20139th century), rendered into early modern English by Newton \u2014 four layers of transmission. Contemplatively, the line is the root of the Correspondence principle: the invitation to read the lower by the higher. Keep all three readings \u2014 textual, historical, contemplative \u2014 in play at once; the tradition's best readers always did."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Calling the Corpus Hermeticum a work of Moses' contemporary: Renaissance readers believed this; Casaubon (1614) and modern scholarship place the treatises in the 1st\u20133rd centuries CE.",
        "Presenting the Emerald Tablet as an ancient Egyptian artifact: the documentary record begins with Arabic and medieval Latin transmission; the tomb-discovery story is legend.",
        "Treating The Kybalion (1908) as an ancient primary source: it is a modern interpretive work of the occult revival.",
        "Reading John Dee's angel magic as disproving his mathematics (or vice versa): before the modern science/esotericism divide, Hermeticism was a live idiom of natural philosophy.",
        "Assuming 'as above, so below' is the tablet's exact wording: it is a summary of the opening maxim; the transmitted texts say 'like that which is above.'"
      ],
      "try_it": "In the Terminal view, type: journal tablet \u2014 copy out the tablet's opening line in your own words, then write one paragraph on what changes when you read 'like' as analogy instead of identity.",
      "id": "cbt-None",
      "domain_id_remapped_from": "texts-and-tradition",
      "merged_from": "cbt-individual/hermetica"
    },
